          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Aldous Huxley's Early Life</strong><br>\nAldous Huxley was born on July 26, 1894, in Godalming, United Kingdom. Coming from a family with a rich intellectual heritage, his early life was marked by a mix of scientific enthusiasm and literary brilliance. He suffered an illness at age 16 that left him partially blind, which ended his hopes of becoming a scientist. Despite this setback, Huxley developed a passion for literature and graduated from Balliol College, Oxford, with a degree in English literature.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Huxley's Literary Career</strong><br>\nAfter Oxford, Huxley began his career in literature. He published his first book, 'Crome Yellow,' in 1921. It was a satirical novel that mocked the pretensions of the literary culture of his time. This set the tone for much of his work, which often combined wit with critique. Huxley wrote several novels, essays, and short stories, becoming a prominent member of the literary elite.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Visionary Works</strong><br>\nHuxley is perhaps best known for his novel 'Brave New World,' published in 1932. The book is a dystopian vision of the future where society is controlled by technology, conditioning, and eugenics. It raised questions about the nature of happiness, freedom, and society. The novel has been praised for its prescient views on the dangers of losing individuality to conformity and state control.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
