          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Themes and Philosophy</strong><br>\nHuxley's writing often explored human potentials and the pursuit of meaning beyond the material world. He was interested in spiritual and philosophical questions, which is reflected in his later works, including 'The Perennial Philosophy,' a study in mysticism. His works repeatedly addressed the struggle to find inner peace and the true essence of being human in an increasingly technological world.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Hollywood and Screenwriting</strong><br>\nIn the 1930s, Huxley moved to Hollywood and worked as a screenwriter. He contributed to several film scripts, though he found the business restrictive. His time in Hollywood influenced his later work, which sometimes reflected the film industry's impact on culture and society.</p>\n\n<p><strong>WWII and Political Thought</strong><br>\nDuring World War II, Huxley's writing took a political turn. His pacifist leanings shaped his essays and novels, like 'Time Must Have a Stop' and 'Island,' which advocated for peace and warned against nationalism and war's destructive nature. He remained a critic of totalitarianism and advocated for humanistic values.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
