          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Psychedelic Exploration</strong><br>\nIn the 1950s, Huxley became interested in the potential of psychedelic drugs for exploring the human mind. He documented his experiences with mescaline in 'The Doors of Perception,' which influenced the counterculture movement of the 1960s. His work in this field made him an early advocate for the therapeutic potential of such substances.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Later Years and Legacy</strong><br>\nHuxley continued writing until his death on November 22, 1963, the same day as the assassination of President John F. Kennedy. His later works reflected his continued interest in philosophical and spiritual issues. Huxley's intellectual contributions extended far beyond his novels; his essays and speeches also had a significant impact on the cultural and philosophical discourse of the 20th century.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Huxley's Influence on Modern Thought</strong><br>\nAldous Huxley left a profound mark on modern thought. His works continue to be studied for their insightful critiques of society, technology, and the pursuit of happiness. He is remembered not just as a writer but as a thinker who dared to envision the future and its challenges. His legacy endures in the continued relevance of his cautionary tales and visionary ideas.</p>\n"
              },
